    "product_id": "17faf5c5-041f-4aad-9002-808e4c37970b",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 241050\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n350 AM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry and tranquil weather will persist through at least the \n  weekend as temperatures gradually warm into a slightly above \n  normal range. \n\n- Periods of breezy conditions over high terrain areas and along \n  the lower Colorado River Valley will be common the next couple \n  days. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nThe weather pattern through the rest of the week will continue to\nsupport fairly progressive systems across the northern tier \nstates with ridging mostly dominating across the Southwestern U.S.\nA bit stronger ridging is set to move through today and this is\nexpected to be enough to push high temperatures back into the\nnormal range for this time of year. For Wednesday, guidance shows\na weak shortwave trough quickly approaching from the northwest,\nbut it is likely to mostly miss the region to the southwest. Even\nif it did move through the region, it is too dry to carry any\nprecipitation chances. It should however bring some breezy winds\nfocused more during the morning hours across the Arizona high\nterrain and across southeast California. The dry air mass that is\nalready in place is forecast to persist with only a period of\nsemi-thick higher level clouds on Wednesday. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nNear perfect weather is expected for Thanksgiving and through the\nholiday weekend as guidance heavily favors an upper level ridge \nbuilding over the region and staying in place through at Sunday. \nH5 heights are forecast to rise to between 582-585dm Thursday into\nFriday before leveling off over the weekend. This would place H5 \nheights well into the 90th percentile of climatology for late \nNovember, while NBM forecast highs rise into the upper 70s across\nthe majority of the lower deserts starting Thursday. These highs \nof 5-8 degrees above normal should continue through the holiday \nweekend, while lows also warm to a few degrees above normal. Aside\nfrom the occasional passing high clouds, anticipate mostly clear \nskies through the period.\n\nModel ensembles then point toward a large Pacific trough setting \nup off the West Coast Sunday into Monday before likely tracking \nthrough our region at some point around next Tuesday and/or \nWednesday. Although still around a week away, models are leaning \ntoward increased precipitation chances by the middle of next week,\nbut so far it does not look like a very strong atmospheric river \nwill impact the Desert Southwest.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1100Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT; and \nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo major weather issues will exist through Monday morning under \noccasional passing cirrus decks. Confidence is very good that \nlight E/NE winds will predominate much of the period across the \nPhoenix metro, with only a brief period of W/NW, or variable wind \ndirections closer to sunset. One caveat is the potential for a \nlight SW wind for a few hours after sunrise at KPHX. For Southeast\nCalifornia terminals, wind directions will generally vary between\nwest and north. Extended periods of variability to nearly calm\nconditions will be common across all the terminals. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER... \nDry and tranquil conditions will continue across the region with\nMinRH's ranging between 15-30% through most of the forecast\nperiod, while cool overnight lows will result in decent to good \novernight recovery each night. Temperatures will warm up to \nslightly above normal starting Wednesday and stay mild through the\nweekend. Outside of periods of marginally breezy conditions over \nAZ high terrain areas and the Lower Colorado River Valley, light \nwinds will be favored. \n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...Updated 11/23 1933Z.\nGila River: A Flood Warning remains in effect from Painted Rock \nDam through the Dateland area due to continued water releases \nupwards of 3000 cfs. Water levels near Dateland continue to rise, \nwith gauge data now showing a depth of just above 7 ft and flow \nexceeding 1100 cfs. At the current rate of rise, the water level \nat the Dateland gauge is expected to reach action stage (8 ft) by \nThursday-Friday. A separate Flood Warning segment remains in \neffect from Dateland to Yuma. These water levels will have the \npotential to flood farm fields and low water crossings along the \nriver downstream from the dam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Whittock/18\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\nHYDROLOGY...Benedict/RW\n",
